    dict(key='11-act-3-figure', kind='figure', title='How the login is written into every request',
         body='basic_header',
         narration='So what does the filter actually read? [[slnc 400]] Before encoding: Ben types his name and password. [[slnc 300]] They are joined by a colon. [[slnc 300]] Ben, colon, ben dash pass. [[slnc 300]] Twelve bytes. [[slnc 500]] Then Base sixty four takes three bytes at a time, and writes each group as four letters. [[slnc 300]] B, E, N, becomes capital Y, small m, capital V, small u. [[slnc 500]] After encoding, the request carries one header: Authorization, Basic, and the sixteen letters. [[slnc 500]] It looks scrambled, but it is not. [[slnc 300]] Base sixty four is an encoding, not encryption. [[slnc 300]] Anyone who sees it decodes it back. [[slnc 300]] So Basic login must only travel over H T T P S.'),
    dict(key='12-act-3', kind='console', title='Act Three — The login on the wire: the HTTP Basic header',
         body='THREE. The login on the wire: the HTTP Basic header.\n  BEFORE ENCODING (what ben types):\n    username ben, password ben-pass\n    joined   ben:ben-pass   (12 bytes)\n  STEP BY STEP (every 3 bytes become 4 letters):\n    ben  ->  YmVu\n    :be  ->  OmJl\n    n-p  ->  bi1w\n    ass  ->  YXNz\n  AFTER ENCODING (what every request carries):\n    Authorization: Basic YmVuOmJlbi1wYXNz\n  anyone can decode it: ben:ben-pass  <- NOT ENCRYPTED',
         narration='The program builds the header step by step. [[slnc 300]] Before encoding, the joined pair. [[slnc 300]] Then four groups of three bytes, each turned into four letters. [[slnc 300]] After encoding, the header. [[slnc 300]] And the last line decodes it back, without any key.'),
    dict(key='13-act-4-figure', kind='figure', title='What BCrypt stores, and how a login is checked',
         body='bcrypt_parts',
         narration="And how is the password checked? [[slnc 400]] Before hashing, B crypt takes three things. [[slnc 300]] Ben's password. [[slnc 300]] A salt, which is random in real use. [[slnc 300]] And a cost of four, which means two to the power four, sixteen rounds. [[slnc 500]] After hashing, the shop stores one line, in four parts. [[slnc 300]] The version. [[slnc 300]] The cost. [[slnc 300]] The salt, in plain sight. [[slnc 300]] And the hash. [[slnc 300]] The password itself is never stored. [[slnc 500]] To check a login, B crypt hashes the typed password again, with the same salt and cost. [[slnc 300]] Ben pass gives exactly the stored hash. [[slnc 300]] Guess gives a completely different one, so the answer is four oh one."),
